                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "The dict_retrieve node takes one value out of a dictionary, by a key you send it.\n\nIt keeps a copy of the last dictionary it received. Whenever a dictionary arrives, or the key changes, it looks the key up and sends the value it finds.\nThat second part matters: you can browse a dictionary you already have by changing the key, without sending the dictionary again.\n\nWhen the key is not in the dictionary nothing is sent.\n\nSYNTAX:\ndict_retrieve\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ndict in:\nThe dictionary to look in. It is kept until the next one arrives.\n\nkey:\nThe key to look up. A message box sends a list even for one word; a one-word list is read as that word, and a longer one as its words joined by spaces.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nvalue out:\nThe value filed under the key.\n\nRELATED:\ndict_extract takes out several values at once, by keys fixed in advance. dict looks up by key in a collection it stores itself."
        },
